  choices = $EXPL$["Section 179 would be more advantageous because it has no income limitation and can create or increase a net operating loss","Bonus depreciation can create or increase an NOL because it has no taxable income limitation, unlike Section 179 which is capped at business income","Both produce identical results because the income limitation applies equally to bonus depreciation and to Section 179 deductions","Neither deduction is available in a loss year because all depreciation methods require the taxpayer to have positive taxable income"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 179 deductions cannot exceed aggregate taxable income from active trades or businesses, producing a $0 current deduction in Morrison''s loss year (excess carries forward). Bonus depreciation under Section 168(k) has no taxable income limitation — 40% of $800,000 produces a $320,000 deduction that deepens the NOL for carryforward.
Wrong (A): This reverses the income limitation rules — Section 179 has the limitation, not bonus depreciation.
Wrong (C): The income limitation applies only to Section 179, not to bonus depreciation.
Wrong (D): Bonus depreciation has no income requirement and is available regardless of the taxpayer''s profitability.$EXPL$
