-- Q2393: Circular 230 [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No penalty because all communications with the OPR are privileged and cannot be used against the practitioner","A written warning with no further consequences beyond the notice being placed in the practitioner''s file","Automatic referral to criminal prosecution under federal obstruction statutes without any additional administrative review","Additional sanctions including enhanced penalties and potential disbarment for obstructing the disciplinary investigation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under 31 CFR Section 10.51(a)(7) (Circular 230), providing false or misleading information to OPR during a disciplinary investigation is itself sanctionable conduct. This can result in enhanced sanctions beyond those for the original misconduct, including disbarment under Section 10.50.
Wrong (A): Communications during OPR investigations are not privileged — there is an affirmative obligation of truthfulness.
Wrong (B): False statements to OPR are a serious violation that can lead to disbarment, not merely a written warning.
Wrong (C): While criminal referral is possible under 18 U.S.C. Section 1001, it is not automatic — the primary consequence is additional administrative sanctions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2393;

-- Q2396: Circular 230 [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No sanction because the error was caused by the commercial tax software, not the CPA personally","Criminal prosecution for tax fraud based on the $15,000 understatement of the client''s liability","Automatic disbarment from practice before the IRS for any error exceeding $10,000 in understatement","Sanctions for failure to exercise due diligence and competence in preparing the client''s return"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Circular 230 Section 10.22, a practitioner must exercise due diligence in preparing tax returns. Relying on outdated software without verifying the applicable tax code constitutes a failure of due diligence, which is sanctionable under Section 10.50 through censure, suspension, or monetary penalties.
Wrong (A): A CPA cannot shift responsibility to software — the practitioner retains the duty to verify accuracy under Section 10.22.
Wrong (B): An unintentional error from outdated software does not rise to the level of willful fraud warranting criminal prosecution.
Wrong (C): Automatic disbarment does not apply here — disbarment is reserved for the most serious willful misconduct under Section 10.51.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2396;

-- Q2398: Circular 230 [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Must ensure the disbarred individual does not participate in IRS practice through the firm in any capacity","Has no obligations since the partner has already left the firm and is no longer associated with it","Must immediately notify all current clients about the former partner''s disbarment from IRS practice","Must dissolve the partnership within 90 days of learning about the former partner''s disbarment order"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Circular 230 Section 10.24 requires practitioners and firms to ensure that disbarred or suspended individuals do not practice before the IRS through the firm. This includes ensuring the individual does not prepare returns, correspond with the IRS, or represent clients through the firm.
Wrong (B): The firm has affirmative obligations under Section 10.24 even if the partner has left — it must prevent any continued involvement in IRS matters.
Wrong (C): There is no blanket requirement to notify all current clients; the obligation is to prevent unauthorized practice.
Wrong (D): Dissolution of the firm is not required — the firm must only ensure the disbarred individual has no IRS practice involvement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2398;

-- Q2408: Professional Ethics in Tax [hard/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The CPA may prepare the current-year return since the prior-year error has no carryforward effect, but must document the client''s refusal to amend","The CPA must refuse to prepare any future returns once any prior-year error is discovered, regardless of its effect on current amounts","The CPA may carry forward the incorrect amounts because the error originated in a year the CPA did not prepare","The CPA must report the client to the IRS before preparing any current-year return that follows a year with known errors"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SSTS No. 6, when a prior-year error has no carryforward effect on the current-year return, the CPA may continue preparing the current return. The CPA should document the client''s refusal to amend and evaluate the relationship. If the error did affect carryforward items (NOLs, basis, credits), the CPA could not sign a return with known incorrect amounts.
Wrong (B): SSTS No. 6 does not require blanket refusal — the CPA''s obligation depends on whether the error affects the current year.
Wrong (C): A CPA cannot knowingly carry forward incorrect amounts regardless of who prepared the prior return.
Wrong (D): There is no obligation to report the client to the IRS — the CPA should advise the client to file an amended return.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2408;

-- Q2410: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The CPA has no responsibility because the software vendor assumes all liability for output errors","The CPA is responsible only if the CPA personally wrote or modified the tax preparation software code","The CPA must review the return for reasonableness and accuracy and is responsible for proper software use","The CPA must independently verify every calculation on the return by hand before filing it"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under SSTS No. 3, a CPA may rely on tax software for mathematical accuracy but retains professional responsibility to review the output for reasonableness, including verifying that limitations, phase-outs, and rates were correctly applied. The CPA is responsible for proper use of the software.
Wrong (A): A CPA cannot delegate professional responsibility to a software vendor — the duty to review remains with the practitioner.
Wrong (B): Responsibility is not contingent on who wrote the software; the CPA must review all output regardless.
Wrong (D): SSTS No. 3 establishes a reasonableness review standard, not a requirement to manually replicate every computation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2410;

-- Q2413: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The CPA should use professional judgment to determine the appropriate form, considering the transaction''s importance and complexity","A CPA must always provide tax advice in writing, signed, dated, and retained in the client file for seven years","A CPA may provide oral advice only for matters involving less than $10,000 in potential tax liability","A CPA must provide tax advice using standardized IRS-approved templates and prescribed formats only"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SSTS No. 7, a CPA may communicate tax advice in any form — written or oral — appropriate to the circumstances. The CPA should use professional judgment considering the transaction''s importance, dollar amounts, complexity, and likelihood of challenge.
Wrong (B): SSTS No. 7 does not require all advice to be in writing — the form is left to professional judgment.
Wrong (C): There is no $10,000 dollar threshold that dictates whether advice must be written or may be oral.
Wrong (D): There are no IRS-mandated templates or prescribed formats for tax advice under SSTS No. 7.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2413;

-- Q2415: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept the $5,000 estimate without question and include it on the return as reported by the client","Require the client to sign a separate indemnification agreement before including any estimated amounts","Refuse to include any amount for business expenses on the return without supporting receipts or records","Make reasonable inquiries about the estimate''s basis and include it only if it appears reasonable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under SSTS No. 4, a CPA may use client estimates when exact data is unavailable, provided the CPA makes reasonable inquiries about the basis and determines the estimate is reasonable under the circumstances, considering the client''s occupation and income level.
Wrong (A): Blindly accepting a round-number estimate without inquiry fails to meet the standard of professional care under SSTS No. 4.
Wrong (B): SSTS No. 4 requires reasonable inquiry, not a separate indemnification agreement from the client.
Wrong (C): Categorical refusal of all estimates lacking receipts is not required — SSTS No. 4 permits reasonable estimates.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2415;

-- Q2417: Professional Ethics in Tax [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The strategy involves significant legal uncertainty and the client could face substantial penalties if the position fails","The amount of tax savings from the strategy is less than $1,000 in total projected benefit","The client has been a long-time client who trusts the CPA''s oral recommendations on complex matters","Written advice is only necessary for transactions that appear on the IRS listed transactions list"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under SSTS No. 7, written advice is particularly important when the matter involves significant legal uncertainty, substantial dollar amounts, or potential penalties. Complex multi-entity strategies warrant written documentation to protect the client''s understanding and the CPA''s professional interests.
Wrong (B): Small tax savings do not trigger the need for written advice — it is significant uncertainty and penalties that do.
Wrong (C): Client trust and relationship length do not reduce the need for written advice on complex, high-risk matters.
Wrong (D): SSTS No. 7 does not limit written advice requirements to listed transactions — the standard applies broadly based on professional judgment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2417;

-- Q2420: Professional Ethics in Tax [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is never acceptable because IRS regulations have the force of law and cannot be challenged by practitioners","Requires pre-approval from the IRS National Office before the position may be taken on any return","Is acceptable only if the taxpayer has previously won on the identical issue in a Tax Court proceeding","May be acceptable if the CPA has a good-faith belief that the regulation does not accurately reflect the law"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under SSTS No. 1 and Circular 230 Section 10.34, a CPA may recommend a position contrary to an IRS regulation if supported by substantial authority (such as case law or statutory language) and a good-faith belief that the regulation does not accurately reflect the law. Disclosure requirements under IRC Section 6662 should be considered.
Wrong (A): Regulations are not immune from challenge — courts can invalidate regulations that exceed statutory authority.
Wrong (B): There is no pre-approval requirement from the IRS National Office for taking positions contrary to regulations.
Wrong (C): A prior Tax Court victory is not required — substantial authority from other sources (statutes, case law) is sufficient.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2420;

-- Q2422: Professional Ethics in Tax [easy/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Report the client to the appropriate licensing authority as required by AICPA ethical standards","Continue preparing the return accurately and advise the client to obtain proper licensing for the business","Include a note on the return disclosing the client''s unlicensed business status to the IRS","Refuse to report the income from the unlicensed business because it derives from improper activity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under SSTS No. 1 and IRC Section 61(a), all income from whatever source is includable in gross income, regardless of whether the underlying activity is licensed. The CPA''s obligation is to report income accurately and advise the client to obtain proper licensing.
Wrong (A): A CPA is not required to report clients to licensing authorities — tax reporting obligations are separate from regulatory compliance.
Wrong (C): There is no requirement to disclose a client''s licensing status on the tax return to the IRS.
Wrong (D): Income from unlicensed activity is still taxable — the CPA cannot refuse to report legally derived income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2422;

-- Q2424: Professional Ethics in Tax [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The CPA may allocate losses however the partners agree, since partnership agreements control all allocations","The CPA must allocate losses strictly in proportion to ownership percentages regardless of the partnership agreement","The CPA should decline the engagement because special allocations disproportionate to ownership are inherently fraudulent","The CPA should evaluate whether the special allocation has substantial economic effect under IRC Section 704(b)"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 704(b), special allocations are permitted only if they have substantial economic effect. Under SSTS No. 1, the CPA must evaluate whether the proposed 90/30 allocation meets the economic effect safe harbor or equivalence test before taking the position on the return.
Wrong (A): Partner agreement alone is insufficient — the allocation must meet the substantial economic effect standard under Section 704(b).
Wrong (B): Strict proportional allocation is not required; special allocations are permitted if they meet the legal standard.
Wrong (C): Special allocations are not inherently fraudulent — they are a legitimate planning tool when properly structured.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2424;

-- Q2428: Tax Procedures [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["One year from the date the return was filed by the taxpayer with the IRS","Five years from the date the return was filed by the taxpayer with the IRS","Three years from the later of the filing date or the due date of the return","Ten years from the date the return was filed by the taxpayer with the IRS"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 6501(a), the IRS generally has three years from the later of the date the return was filed or the due date (including extensions) to assess additional tax. If filed early, the statute runs from the original due date.
Wrong (A): A one-year period is far too short — the standard assessment period is three years under Section 6501(a).
Wrong (B): Five years is not the general statute; the six-year period applies only when gross income is understated by more than 25%.
Wrong (D): The ten-year period applies to tax collection under Section 6502, not to assessment of additional tax.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2428;

-- Q2429: Tax Procedures [medium/L3]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The three-year statute applies regardless of the omission amount because no provision extends the period for income omissions","Any omission of any amount automatically extends the statute to six years because all reporting errors receive identical treatment","The $30,000 omission exceeds 25% of reported gross income, triggering the six-year statute; a $20,000 omission would not","The 25% threshold reduces the statute to one year rather than extending it, giving smaller omissions longer IRS scrutiny"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 6501(e)(1)(A) extends the assessment period from three to six years when the taxpayer omits more than 25% of gross income stated on the return. Here, $30,000/$100,000 = 30%, exceeding the 25% threshold. A $20,000 omission (20%) would fall below the threshold, keeping the standard three-year period.
Wrong (A): Section 6501(e) specifically provides the extended six-year period for substantial omissions of gross income.
Wrong (B): The six-year extension requires exceeding the 25% threshold — not every omission triggers it.
Wrong (D): This reverses the effect entirely. Exceeding the threshold extends the period; it does not reduce it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2429;

-- Q2439: Tax Procedures [medium/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The later of three years from the filing date or two years from the date the tax was paid to the IRS","One year from the date of overpayment, measured from the original payment to the Treasury","Five years from the due date of the return, regardless of when the return was actually filed","There is no time limit for filing refund claims because the government should return overpayments"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 6511, a refund claim must be filed by the later of three years from the filing date or two years from the date the tax was paid. The refund amount is limited based on which window the claim falls within — three years of payments or two years of payments.
Wrong (B): One year from the overpayment date is not a recognized statute of limitations period for refund claims under Section 6511.
Wrong (C): The statute is three years from filing or two years from payment — not five years from the due date.
Wrong (D): Refund claims are subject to strict time limits under Section 6511; there is no unlimited filing period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2439;

-- Q2443: Tax Procedures [hard/L2]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is calculated as a flat 5% of the total underpayment amount owed for the entire tax year","Does not apply if the total tax owed when filing the return is less than $10,000","Can be waived only if the taxpayer has never previously underpaid estimated taxes in any prior year","Is calculated using the federal short-term rate plus 3 points, applied to each quarterly underpayment period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 6654, the estimated tax penalty is computed as interest at the federal short-term rate plus 3 percentage points, calculated for each quarter separately from the quarterly due date to the earlier of payment or filing deadline. Safe harbors exist (90% of current year or 100%/110% of prior year tax).
Wrong (A): The penalty is not a flat 5% — it is an interest-based calculation that varies by quarter and rate.
Wrong (B): There is no $10,000 exemption threshold; the penalty applies based on quarterly underpayment amounts and safe harbor tests.
Wrong (C): The penalty may be waived for casualty, disaster, or unusual circumstances — not solely based on the taxpayer''s prior compliance history.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2443;

-- Q2450: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A manifestation of contractual intent, definite and certain terms, and communication to the offeree","A written document signed by both parties before any performance begins under the agreement","Consideration exchanged at the time the offer is made to demonstrate the offeror''s seriousness","Approval by a neutral third party to confirm the offer''s terms are fair and enforceable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 24, a valid offer requires (1) present contractual intent, (2) definite and certain terms, and (3) communication to the offeree. Garrett''s proposal fails because ''a reasonable price to be determined later'' lacks definiteness in a material term.
Wrong (B): Offers need not be in writing unless the Statute of Frauds applies — oral offers can be valid under common law.
Wrong (C): Consideration is required for a binding contract, not for a valid offer. An offer precedes the exchange of consideration.
Wrong (D): No third-party approval is required for a valid offer under common law contract principles.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2450;
